Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||08-21-2000||01:29 AM||||theres a company near me that will take the rod that goes from the clutch pedal to the master cylinder and thread it with a sleeve, so you can adjust the throw. i don't have any idea on pricing (i don't need one) but is anyone interested in me finding out a price?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||08-21-2000||08:16 AM||||i used a threaded rod from my slave cylinder to my transmission while i was trying to work out a clutch problem. when i finaly gave up and took it to the shop (independent, but only works on gm cars. has a rep for being vette specialist and about the only place that i would say i trust what they tell me). ended up needing a clutch any way and was told that the system is not adjustable. had to find my old rod and replace the lever on tranny. i had drilled a hole through it for the threaded rod. i can only guess that an adjustable rod from the pedal to the  master cyl. wouldn't be any better. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||08-21-2000||08:41 AM||||There are like a half million posts on the clutch master/slave problem. The bottom line is, you can alter the length of the rod, or the distance of the slave from the arm, or dance on the roof of your house in the nude chanting ancient ceremonial Indian songs. They'll ALL afford the same result. None.<BR>All you do is relocate the piston inside the slave cylinder. The BEST suggestins I've heard (read) on this topic, in order to gain maximum clutch movement are these:<P>Ensure the slave is properly aligned with the throwout arm. The mounting bracket is subject to bending and flexing I guess. I believe Rodney Dickman sells a steel repalcement that's more solid.<P>Make sure the system is properly bled, and no possibility of air in the system exists. This includes bleeding air fron the fitting by the master cylinder.<P>Verify your pedal isn't bent. Repair it or replace it if it is.<P>Check the rod that connects the master cylinder to the pedal, and make sure it's not worn where it attaches to the pedal arm.<P>If all that is good, and your clutch is still not very good, congratualtions, you are OFFICIALLY a Fiero owner!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>-Taiji<P>ps:  Do a search for "clutch" and you'll find a ga-zillion posts, and all the info you could ever want about this.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSTETZ||08-21-2000||09:18 PM||||well, the reason that i brought this up is because i know i would like to be able to adjust the point at which my clutch engages/releases for comfort. i was not proposing this as a fix to the evil clutch problem so prevalent in our cars, just something that could make driving more comfortable<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673"> ||||reg||1
